A||N||3||Fiero_Freak||Rear Caliper e-brake||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero_Freak||12-27-2001||01:57 PM||||   What is the adjustment procedure for the e-brake portion of new caliper installation.  Are you suppose to wind down the head that the lever attaches to?<P>   My e-brake just doesn't seem to work that well anymore.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg">  <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-27-2001||06:15 PM||||Sounds like it is back in one piece and no more adjustments may be made at the caliper.<BR>First make sure that the pads contact the rotor when the brake PEDAL is depressed. After insuring the pads have contact the e-brake is adjusted at the coupler at the rear of the cradle below the muffler (84-87) or between the front crosover angle steel of the cradle for 88's.||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||12-28-2001||05:30 AM||||All the adjustment is at the calipers. When you apply the park brake, the lever turns a ratchet screw and pushes out the caliper. If that is gummed up sometimes the piston will retract too far giving you a low pedal too. If all else is right then you probably have a bad cable.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||12-28-2001||12:24 PM||||Loosen the cable first - it will make your life easier.  The adjusting nut is under the rear cradle part where the e-brake cables (left / right / front) are joined together.<P>Once it is loose, you're ready to do the caliper.  Yes, the screw on the caliper (that the lever attaches to) turns as you pull up the e-brake.  Counter-clockwise (If I remember correctly) will compress the piston in the caliper.  What you need to do is make sure that the piston screw is unscrewed (pads tight) on the rotor. I usually do that with a pair of pliers to make sure it's as far as it will go.<P>Once you do this, the hexagonal-shaped hole in the lever should slide over the screw on the caliper.  No, they do not always line up.  Loosen the screw very slightly(releasing the pressure off the pads-to-rotor) until the hexagonal hole fits the screw properly.<P>It sounds not too hard but lining this up can be a bit frustrating and time consuming.  One small problem you may run into is the spring.  In order to slide the lever hexagonal hole onto the screw, the spring has to be installed and compress slightly.  When I did mine, I took 3 pieces of electrical wire (stripped for 'slimness').  When the spring was on a counter (off the caliper) I fed the wires through the spring.  Then I twisted them with a pair of pliers, thus compressing the spring.  Once compressed enough, I installed it, later cutting the wires and removing them.  It sure made things a lot easier.<P>Once the caliper, lever, spring and cable are installed, don't forget to adjust the cable underneath the car.<P>I hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 12-28-2001).]||||reg||1
